# witness-alert.conf.example
# Copy to ~/.witness-alert.conf on the machine that runs the witness.
# This file holds EVERY site-specific value, which is the point: the script
# itself is byte-identical to the copy in this repository, so `git status` in
# the clone is a true statement about what is deployed.
#
# Not in the repo, and must not be: it names a real mailbox.

# Where the witness repository is checked out.
WITNESS_REPO="$HOME/1f916-witness"

# Where alerts go, and who they come from. NO DEFAULTS ON PURPOSE -- the script
# refuses to run without both, because an alarm addressed to nowhere reports
# success, sends nothing, and looks healthy forever.
WITNESS_ALERT_TO="you@example.org"
WITNESS_ALERT_FROM="alerts@example.org"

# Optional. The registry row id, used only to label the subject line:
#   set   -> "[1F916] witness #12 needs attention on <host>"
#   unset -> "[1F916] witness needs attention on <host>"
#WITNESS_ID="12"

# Optional thresholds.
#   WITNESS_STALE_HOURS        newest countersignature older than this -> STALE
#   WITNESS_LOG_STALE_MINUTES  witness.log untouched this long -> NOT RUNNING
# Defaults 3 and 90 suit an hourly witness. Set them from the row's OBSERVED
# cadence, not from what the cron line claims: those are different facts.
#WITNESS_STALE_HOURS="3"
#WITNESS_LOG_STALE_MINUTES="90"

# Optional. State file recording "already told" for this incident.
#WITNESS_ALERT_STATE="$HOME/.witness-alert.state"
